  abstract = {The estimation of exchange integrals in metals and alloys from first-principle calculations yields Curie temperature (TC), which is not in agreement with the experiments. The microscopic exchange interactions of the bcc and fcc FeNi alloys are predicted by training large datasets of random magnetization curves simulated using the atomic scale Monte Carlo method with regression models to accurately reproduce the experimental TC. The estimation of Fe-rich bcc alloys reveals predominant Heisenberg-like Ruderman-Kittel-Kasuya-Yosida (RKKY) type oscillations in Fe--Fe interactions, similar to the behavior in bcc Fe. Furthermore, frustration in Fe--Fe and Fe--Ni/Ni--Fe couplings of fcc Invar alloy results in the reduction of TC. Additionally, the temperature dependence of cubic anisotropy is calculated using constrained Monte Carlo algorithm, and the validity of Callen-Callen 10th power law is demonstrated, which shows large deviations of scaling exponent L{$\ll$}10 near permalloy composition attributing to dispersion of magnetic easy axis of Fe and Ni.},
